 Run the tests with `npm test` on Node.js 22.18 or later (with built-in TypeScript support).
+
+## JSON Patch
+
+`applyPatch(document: unknown, patch: unknown): unknown`, exported from `src/index.ts`, applies an
+[RFC 6902 JSON Patch](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6902) and returns a new JSON value. It supports
+the `add`, `remove`, `replace`, `move`, `copy`, and `test` operations. It strictly validates operation
+members and JSON Pointer paths, and throws an `Error` for an invalid patch or a failed operation.
+
+```ts
+import { applyPatch } from './src/index.js';
+
+const document = { users: [{ name: 'Ada' }] };
+const updated = applyPatch(document, [
+  { op: 'add', path: '/users/-', value: { name: 'Lin' } },
+  { op: 'replace', path: '/users/0/name', value: 'Ada Lovelace' },
+]);
+// document is unchanged; updated has both changes.
+```
+
+The document and each `value` member must be JSON values. The patch is an array of operation objects;
+additional operation members are ignored as specified by RFC 6902.
